\details{
\subsection{Study scope and background}{

\code{.object} is the scope \code{U}; narrow it first with \code{\link[=faers_restrict]{faers_restrict()}}. Both
comparison modes only ever look inside \code{U}.
\itemize{
\item \code{.mode = "background"}: every group \code{G} is compared with the rest of \code{U},
i.e. the 2x2 is \code{a} = reports of \code{G} with the event, \code{c} = reports of
\verb{U \\ G} with the event, over \verb{n = |U|}. Each analysis uses the same \code{U},
reports are de-duplicated per table, and every table satisfies
\verb{a + b + c + d == |U|}. Identical to
\code{faers_phv_table(.object = G, .full = U)}.
\item \code{.mode = "pairwise"}: the background is a \emph{selected} second group, so
\verb{n = |G1| + |G2|} and the denominator changes with every pair. Identical to
\code{faers_phv_table(.object = G1, .object2 = G2)} once overlap is reconciled.
\code{.comparisons} must be given explicitly; \code{NULL} aborts instead of silently
running every pair, because per-pair denominators invite uncorrected
multiple comparisons.
}

The two modes are marked on every result row (\code{mode}, \code{comparison_id},
\code{background_id}) and summarised in \code{comparisons}.
}

\subsection{Overlap}{

\code{.overlap} is forwarded to \code{\link[=faers_group_ids]{faers_group_ids()}}. \code{"keep"} (default) is
correct for multi-group background comparisons: each group is compared
against the scope, not against another group, and the shared reports are
reported in \code{group_overlaps}. Before a \emph{pairwise} comparison the two groups
must not overlap; with \code{"keep"} and a non-empty intersection the pair aborts
with the count, so pass \code{"exclude"} (remove the intersection from both, the
removed counts are recorded) or \code{"repartition"}/\code{.split = "joint"} (turn the
intersection into its own group).
}

\subsection{Joint-group contrast}{

\code{faers_group_ids(.split = "joint")} splits two drugs into \code{A-only},
\code{B-only}, \code{A+B} and \code{Neither} such that the four sets are mutually exclusive
and sum to \code{U}. \code{faers_compare()} accepts such a split either by being given
the four groups as \code{.groups} or by \code{.split = "joint"} directly. The result is
a \strong{descriptive comparison and disproportionality analysis of co-reported
drugs}: it is not a formal drug-interaction test, and \code{Neither} exists
precisely so that the four-way partition stays reportable.
}

\subsection{Display subsets}{

\code{.top_n}, when set, only truncates the \emph{display} layer (\code{event_profile} and
\code{matrices}) using the rule "per group, keep the top N events by report count,
then take the union across groups"; ties break on the event label so the
result is deterministic. Truncated results are flagged with
\code{attr(., "display_subset") = TRUE} and recorded in
\code{metadata$display_subset}. \code{signal_results} always keeps every row so that
downstream filtering sees \code{status} instead of missing data.
}
